00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.110
And here we are.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:06.550
MELANIE WARRICK:
And here we are.

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:08.216
And so that's where
that idea came from.

00:05:08.216 --> 00:05:10.490
And I think I read that
it's Perceptual CI was what

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:11.910
it was originally named as.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:12.455
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Oh wow.

00:05:12.455 --> 00:05:14.780
You really went into the
depths of the history there.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:15.260
MELANIE WARRICK: Took a minute.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:16.843
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Yes,
it was originally

00:05:16.843 --> 00:05:19.010
called Perceptual CI,
which probably makes sense

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:21.650
if you're an engineer and
is terrible if you're not.

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:23.374
So my best friend
actually-- we already

00:05:23.374 --> 00:05:25.790
had the mascot at the time,
which you see on our Home page

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:28.520
is Percy the
Perceptual Porcupine.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:30.140
And the color scheme is purple.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:30.930
So a little bit of alliteration.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.790
MELANIE WARRICK: Where did
the porcupine come from?

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:33.090
MIKE FOTINAKIS: I don't know.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:34.250
It just alliterated
with the rest of the--

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:34.790
MARK MANDEL: Alliterated?

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:34.860
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Yeah.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:35.780
MELANIE WARRICK:
It is kind of cute.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:36.320
MARK MANDEL: That makes sense.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:36.980
MIKE FOTINAKIS: And
then, my best friend

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:38.700
said, why don't you just
call the thing Percy?

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:39.540
And I was like, oh.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.497
Well, Percy.io was
available at the time.

00:05:41.497 --> 00:05:43.580
So we grabbed it, and the
rest is sort of history.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:45.320
I spent a day
refactoring the code

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:48.044
to get rid of all the Perceptual
references, and here we are.

00:05:48.044 --> 00:05:48.710
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:50.750
So just so I get this
straight in my head.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:53.050
This is a continuous
integration system

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:56.390
that enables me to see UI bugs.

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:57.710
How does it see UI bugs?

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:58.850
How does that work?

00:05:58.850 --> 00:05:59.683
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Yes.

00:05:59.683 --> 00:06:03.680
So Percy's entire goal
is to really give you

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.934
confidence in every visual
change you're making.

00:06:06.934 --> 00:06:09.350
And even in changes you're
making that you might not think

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:09.880
are visual.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:11.510
So there's a bunch
of times when you're

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:12.551
making a back-end change.

00:06:12.551 --> 00:06:15.691
And for some reason, it
manifests as a front-end bug.

00:06:15.691 --> 00:06:17.440
And you might not catch
that if you're not

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:19.620
doing QA on the front end.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:21.620
If you're not looking
specifically at that page.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:23.960
If you're relying on
your own memory to like,

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:26.240
remember what the page
looked like before, this

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:28.830
is why things leak through
to production all the time.

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.100
We just miss the stuff.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:30.600
So yeah.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.480
So the way that Percy
approaches this problem

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:37.290
is to take a visual comparison
of what the state was before,

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:40.190
and then what the state
is after the code change.

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:43.280
And we do this in a
truly continuous way.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:44.780
So I like to say--

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:46.280
we've sort of gone through a
couple of different iterations

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:47.738
of these phrases,
but I like to say

00:06:47.738 --> 00:06:49.520
continuous visual
validation, or like

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.440
continuous visual integration.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.200
So it's really meant
to bring the process

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.390
of doing this as early in
the software development

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:57.800
cycle as possible.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:59.740
So the goal and the
way that Percy works

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:01.490
is I should be able
to make a code change,

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:03.470
and then immediately
look at Percy and see,

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:05.690
oh, I affected 900 pages.

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:06.720
And I didn't mean to.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.600
I only meant to
affect one, right?

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.480
And so what we'll show you
is like, a visual comparison.

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:12.770
What we call a visual diff.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:15.920
And these visual diffs are
basically just a comparison

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:18.367
of two different images
without any context

00:07:18.367 --> 00:07:20.200
necessarily about what
the images are about.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:23.189
They're just like, these pixels
have changed from these pixels

00:07:23.189 --> 00:07:24.980
here on the left and
master to these pixels

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:27.065
here on the right in
your pull request.

00:07:27.065 --> 00:07:28.620
MELANIE WARRICK: I know when
I've worked with GitHub before.

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:31.149
I mean, I go in and look at
the diffs between the code

00:07:31.149 --> 00:07:31.940
and what's changed.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:33.650
And I can see they
do like color-coding.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:34.760
And you can look from--

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:36.260
one side is what
it was originally

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:38.580
versus the other side
is what I've changed.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.790
So it's that idea.

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:44.120
But instead, you're seeing the
actual visual representations

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:45.222
of every single page.

00:07:45.222 --> 00:07:46.430
MIKE FOTINAKIS: That's right.

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.160
So that's exactly
what we want to do

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:52.171
and the workflow process that
we want to allow, like designers

00:07:52.171 --> 00:07:53.420
and engineers to work through.

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:55.040
Because you know, you
go to GitHub right now,

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:56.750
and you look on the left
and you look on the right.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:59.291
And you're like, OK, this code
is changing from this to this.

00:07:59.291 --> 00:08:01.490
And code review gives you
a lot of nice benefits

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:05.011
of like catching bugs from your
teammates who can understand

00:08:05.011 --> 00:08:06.260
the code and the architecture.

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:08.010
Or they can say like, oh, this--

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:09.510
you don't need to
build it this way.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:10.940
We have this other system
that does that now.

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:11.773
You don't need that.

00:08:11.773 --> 00:08:14.150
So you get a lot of these
benefits out of the code review

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:16.160
process that aren't
necessarily like something

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:18.118
you would ever get from
a static analysis tool,

00:08:18.118 --> 00:08:19.550
like a linter or something else.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:21.650
And I think the
same exact workflow

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:23.877
and processes apply
to visual testing.

00:08:23.877 --> 00:08:25.460
You want to be able
to say as a human,

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:28.100
like yes, these are the
visual changes we wanted.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:29.820
We wanted those visual changes.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:33.784
And whoops, we didn't mean for
this one to change that page.

00:08:33.784 --> 00:08:35.659
That modal on that page
and that deep setting

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:37.190
state on your
billing page changed.

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:39.774
And like, you didn't even
mean to do that because you

00:08:39.774 --> 00:08:40.940
weren't affecting that page.

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:43.398
And you didn't look at it when
you were making that change.

00:08:43.398 --> 00:08:45.869
So yeah, we want to
give you confidence

00:08:45.869 --> 00:08:47.660
that you know exactly
what you're changing.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:49.421
In the same way that
a code diff gives you

00:08:49.421 --> 00:08:51.920
confidence to know what you're
changing in your application,

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:53.960
like your test suites give
you confidence to know.

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:55.340
MARK MANDEL: So if
you're a developer

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:57.256
and you want to use
Percy, how does that look?

00:08:57.256 --> 00:08:58.819
Is this a tool
that I run locally?

00:08:58.819 --> 00:09:00.860
Is it something I can
integrate with a CI system,

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:01.924
like Travis or Jenkins?

00:09:01.924 --> 00:09:02.840
What can I do with it?

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:03.600
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Yeah, exactly.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:05.510
So we integrate with
CI systems right now.

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:08.874
So what you do is you plug
Percy into your test suites

00:09:08.874 --> 00:09:10.040
that you're already writing.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:11.730
You have these tests that
are like, visit this page,

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:13.160
click these buttons,
do this thing.

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:14.243
Or like, these components.

00:09:14.243 --> 00:09:16.530
We want to test these
components in these states.

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.530
You integrate with your
CI, and then you push up

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:20.390
a pull request to GitHub.

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:23.000
And then as your CI runs,
it's pushing stuff to Percy.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:26.270
And we've actually built a
full, like rendering environment

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.719
on top of Google Cloud
Platform that is this rendering

00:09:28.719 --> 00:09:31.010
farm that's performing these
renders in the background,

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:34.490
doing these screenshots, and
handling all the weird edge

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:36.740
cases that go into that,
which we can talk about.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:40.200
And then, we just notate
the GitHub PR and say, hey,

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.440
there's 15 visual diffs
here that need review.

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:44.910
And that might be right
next to your green check

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:48.170
mark in GitHub that says,
2,000 unit test pass.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:50.470
Functionally, everything
is ready to go.

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:51.470
You're great here.

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:53.190
But then, Percy catches
it and says, hey,

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:55.310
you're about to launch
some visual changes.

00:09:55.310 --> 00:09:57.784
Do you want these visual
changes, yes or no?

00:09:57.784 --> 00:09:58.700
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:09:58.700 --> 00:09:59.930
And then, they can
basically go through

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:01.780
and do that review
side to side, and then

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:04.060
see and accept accordingly?

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:05.230
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Exactly.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:07.930
So that's a real goal, is
like give you a visual review

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:09.139
process.

00:10:09.139 --> 00:10:10.930
MELANIE WARRICK: Like
you are in the GitHub

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:12.940
with the diffs between coding.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:14.860
Are you also able to go
in and directly inside

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:18.490
the visual make
comments or say, here's

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:19.930
a spot that you might miss?

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:21.370
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Yeah, that's
definitely on our roadmap.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:22.480
It's not something
we do right now.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:24.230
You can sort of comment
in GitHub and say,

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:26.620
I noticed this thing in
Percy was wrong at the time.

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:29.230
Or, you changed this page
and you didn't mean to.

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:31.740
But yeah, commenting
and that kind of like--

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:34.300
that's sort of to the
intersectionality thing again.

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:38.440
If we can help designers
and developers and PMs

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:39.890
facilitate all of
these workflows.

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:41.598
And you can say like,
hey, we need our PM

00:10:41.598 --> 00:10:42.922
to sign-off on this change.

00:10:42.922 --> 00:10:44.380
That's totally
something you should

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:46.088
be doing on the visual
side of your site.

00:10:46.088 --> 00:10:48.860
And like, on the changes that
you're making at that level.

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:50.729
So we sort of have a
pretty basic workflow

00:10:50.729 --> 00:10:51.520
for that right now.

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:53.650
We try to keep it really
simple, so you can just iterate

00:10:53.650 --> 00:10:54.760
and improve things very easily.

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:56.470
But more complex
workflows are definitely

00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:58.390
on the roadmap of
things we want to do.

00:10:58.390 --> 00:10:58.930
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:10:58.930 --> 00:10:59.430
All right.

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:01.050
So let's get under the hood.

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:03.600
I want to hear about how
this thing actually works.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:04.870
MIKE FOTINAKIS: OK.

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:07.710
Well, I give a 45-minute
tech talk on it,

00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:09.050
so what pieces would you like?

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:10.247
MARK MANDEL: All right.

00:11:10.247 --> 00:11:11.830
I'm guessing the
most interesting part

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:13.612
is the image
analysis part of it.

00:11:13.612 --> 00:11:15.070
I'm willing to
guess, I don't know.

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:15.967
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Sure.

00:11:15.967 --> 00:11:17.300
MARK MANDEL: How does that work?

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:19.660
I'm guessing that's a fairly
computationally-intensive

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:20.419
workload?

00:11:20.419 --> 00:11:21.460
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Well, OK.

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:24.710
So yeah, a lot of it is pretty
computationally-intensive,

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:26.740
but it's probably not
the part that you expect

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:28.280
is computationally-intensive.

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:30.640
So basically, in the
talk that I give,

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:33.400
I basically show you like,
what is a visual diff

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:34.230
in the first place?

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:35.320
What is a visual diff?

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:37.000
And I just show you two images.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:40.254
And then, a visual diff is just
the difference between these.

00:11:40.254 --> 00:11:41.920
Computationally, what
is the difference?

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:44.030
What's the delta between
these two images?

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:48.430
So it's basically a transparent
PNG with red pixels on it.

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:50.410
And the red pixels tell
you which pixels have

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:52.095
changed between the two states.

00:11:52.095 --> 00:11:54.220
So like these pixels have
changed between these two

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:55.280
states.

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:58.180
So generating those
visual diffs is actually

00:11:58.180 --> 00:12:00.520
one of the cheapest
parts of the process.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:03.490
You can literally run, like an
ImageMagick compare command,

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:06.306
and give it two images,
and give it a diff output.

00:12:06.306 --> 00:12:08.680
And then, you open up the diff
and that's the difference.

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:11.140
And you can apply a bunch of
like, translucency and effects

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:12.120
and stuff, like--

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:13.870
as deep you can go
into ImageMagick there.

00:12:13.870 --> 00:12:14.395
Or [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:14.395 --> 00:12:15.740
MARK MANDEL: Bevels,
and like, gradients.

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:16.090
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Yeah.

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:18.880
You can rewrite this in Go as
your own like PNG interpreter

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:19.640
thing.

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:21.160
Do whatever you want.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:23.380
The hard part about this
system- and the part

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:26.650
that we rely a lot on own
infrastructure, and on GCP

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:29.470
to do and to scale for,
is the actual rendering

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:30.580
of the screenshots.

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:33.570
So there's a bunch of
problems that come along

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:35.050
with rendering screenshots.

00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:38.530
Partially because browsers
are just super-complicated

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:40.030
nowadays.

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:41.530
The source code for
Chrome, I think,

00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:46.110
is as large or almost as
large as the Linux kernel now.

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:47.380
It's massive.

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:49.150
And so these things--

00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:50.962
we want to keep
browsers developing.

00:12:50.962 --> 00:12:52.420
We want to keep
them really modern,

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:55.027
but they come along with all
of these rendering differences.

00:12:55.027 --> 00:12:56.860
For example, the simple
one you can think of

00:12:56.860 --> 00:12:58.130
is like animations.

00:12:58.130 --> 00:13:00.497
It's like, what happens if
an animation is on a page?

00:13:00.497 --> 00:13:02.080
Well, if you take a
visual diff of it,

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:05.410
you might get just a random
frame of the animation as

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:08.690
compared to the previous
random frame of the animation.

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:10.580
So what we do in
Percy, for example,

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:13.420
is we freeze the
CSS of the page,

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:15.010
or the CSS animations
of the page.

00:13:15.010 --> 00:13:17.775
We inject some CSS that tries
to aggressively turn off

00:13:17.775 --> 00:13:19.900
all of those animations,
so that we can freeze them

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:21.190
in a particular state.

00:13:21.190 --> 00:13:23.789
Or like, we freeze animated
GIFs so that they don't progress

00:13:23.789 --> 00:13:24.830
through their animations.

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:26.538
So we're just looking
at the first frame,

00:13:26.538 --> 00:13:28.750
so we can make these renders
really deterministic.

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:30.790
That's the easy one
in a lot of ways.

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:32.380
The harder ones
are like, did you

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:35.290
know that GPUs don't
actually guarantee

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:37.420
that if you divide
two numbers, they will

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:39.310
be the same answer in the end?

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:40.360
Things like that.

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:43.300
If you compile software with
different optimization flags,

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:44.050
you might get--

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:46.619
GPUs might execute the
instructions differently.

00:13:46.619 --> 00:13:48.910
So like, if you're rendering
a gradient on one machine.

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:50.870
If it's like a
GPU-rendered gradient,

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:53.680
and you render that exact same
gradient on another machine,

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:55.880
they might not be pixel
perfectly accurate.

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:57.670
Or like, a little
bit of an easier one.

00:13:57.670 --> 00:13:58.940
So fonts.

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:02.900
If you upgrade the fonts of the
system that you're working on,

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:04.960
you might get weird, like
anti-aliasing artifacts

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:06.400
around the edges of the fonts.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:09.070
If Chrome decides to make the
rendering better, which they

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:10.600
do all the time,
then you might get

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:13.150
weird anti-aliasing
artifacts, or weird edges

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:14.222
of different tiles.

00:14:14.222 --> 00:14:15.680
Or just improved
rendering overall.

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:17.650
Or a new CSS feature.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:20.020
So one of the big parts
of what we do at Percy

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:22.140
is we try to handle
this process for you

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:25.200
and give you a really
deterministic rendering

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:27.700
environment that handles
all of the complexities

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:29.210
of visual testing.

00:14:29.210 --> 00:14:32.230
So for example, we freeze
the fonts of the system

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:34.810
and handle this in
a containerized way.

00:14:34.810 --> 00:14:36.850
We freeze the
animations on the pages.

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:40.300
We handle the browser
upgrade process for you

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:42.640
and try to keep everything
modern under the stack.

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:44.260
So the sort of naive
solution to this

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:46.960
is, I'm running my CI system.

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:49.840
And I just tell it like,
hey, render me a screenshot

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:52.690
and put it to disk, or put
it in some artifact directory

00:14:52.690 --> 00:14:55.532
and keep the screenshots
across the CI runs.

00:14:55.532 --> 00:14:57.490
That's kind of how the
first iteration of Percy

00:14:57.490 --> 00:14:58.489
actually started, right?

00:14:58.489 --> 00:15:00.010
We were running
a Jenkins install

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:02.468
and it is just, like dumping
screenshots and running diffs.

00:15:02.468 --> 00:15:05.950
And then, you kind of can sort
of well what's happening there.

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:09.384
If you do that, then
what happens is you'll

00:15:09.384 --> 00:15:11.800
end up generating a bunch of
screenshots that end up being

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:13.840
slightly different over time.

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:17.030
And for reasons that you
can't actually control.

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:17.710
So that's one.

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:20.180
So like determinism, is
like the big problem.

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:22.850
And then, the other second
big problem is performance.

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:27.190
It can take 15, 20, 30
seconds to tell a browser

00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:31.250
to actually render a full
page PNG of the image.

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:34.606
So if you end up doing a
hundred of those in a CI run,

00:15:34.606 --> 00:15:35.980
which is not that
many UI states,

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:38.290
you might add 30 minutes
to your test suite,

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:39.620
which nobody wants to do.

00:15:39.620 --> 00:15:41.495
And it's kind of like
a horrible thing, which

00:15:41.495 --> 00:15:44.230
I think this performance is
like the big, big reason why

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:46.813
visual testing is not something
that every engineering team is

00:15:46.813 --> 00:15:47.540
doing right now.

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:49.120
So the way that
Percy handles that

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:52.790
is by just making it completely
out of band of your CI suite.

00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:55.330
We add basically no
overhead to your CI suite

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:59.440
at all, because all we do is
we send up these DOM snapshots.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.680
We capture the state of the
page that you've rendered

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:04.120
in your testing environment.

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:06.700
So like, you have a test
that visits some page, clicks

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:08.860
a drop-down, and
then some JavaScript

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:11.590
is executed in Chrome
Headless, or something.

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:13.600
So then, we take the
DOM state of that page

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:15.940
along with the development
assets around it,

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:17.980
send them up to Percy,
and then render them

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:19.960
in our custom rendering farm.

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:23.080
So that allows us to, number
1, parallelize the entire thing

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:25.780
aggressively so we can
finish very quickly.

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:29.710
And really, truly live up to
our goal of being continuous.

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:31.390
And then, 2, it
allows us to control

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:34.690
for all of these variables
where you can't really

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:36.740
deal with those rendering
differences in your CI

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:37.240
environment.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:39.340
And you wouldn't really want to.

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:41.440
So that sort of model
and that architecture

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:42.880
has given us a
lot of flexibility

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:44.710
to really make
Percy try to live up

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:46.987
to this continuous
part of our goal.

00:16:46.987 --> 00:16:49.570
And really, bring this process
really early on in the software

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:50.710
development lifecycle.

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:51.460
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:53.280
I have so many questions.

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:54.352
MELANIE WARRICK: You go.

00:16:54.352 --> 00:16:56.060
I've got questions,
but I want you to go.

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:56.726
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:16:56.726 --> 00:16:59.675
Let's start with, what's the
Google Cloud Platform products

00:16:59.675 --> 00:17:00.649
that you're using here?

00:17:00.649 --> 00:17:01.690
You mentioned containers.

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:03.100
You sort of buried
the lead there

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:05.558
a little bit about-- it sounds
like that's maybe how you're

00:17:05.558 --> 00:17:07.450
running the actual browsers?

00:17:07.450 --> 00:17:08.079
I'm not sure.

00:17:08.079 --> 00:17:10.720
But what does that flow look
like through the products

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:11.220
as you go?

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:12.678
MIKE FOTINAKIS: So
yeah, we've been

00:17:12.678 --> 00:17:15.310
using Google Cloud Platform from
the beginning for everything.

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:17.200
And actually, Percy
grew up in a world

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:20.410
where containers were already
existing in some ways.

00:17:20.410 --> 00:17:23.829
I sort of dealt with like early,
early docker bugs and that sort

00:17:23.829 --> 00:17:25.000
of like early pain there.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:28.900
But glad that we sort of bet
on that early on, because that

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:32.080
has just allowed us to
scale this infrastructure so

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:34.000
much more easily had
we done something else.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:35.710
So yeah, everything
is containerized.

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:38.042
And that's how our full
deployment pipeline works,

00:17:38.042 --> 00:17:40.000
and how we update our
rendering environments is

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.410
pushing these new containers
and pushing new versions.

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:44.584
We just recently,
actually, migrated off of--

00:17:44.584 --> 00:17:46.000
I don't know if
you even remember,

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:47.664
like container-VMs from--

00:17:47.664 --> 00:17:48.580
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yeah.

00:17:48.580 --> 00:17:49.413
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Yes.

00:17:49.413 --> 00:17:51.760
So before Google
Kubernetes engine even.

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:53.500
It was like a
version of Kubernetes

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:54.880
that kind of ran
on a single node

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:56.463
and didn't tell you
it was Kubernetes,

00:17:56.463 --> 00:17:59.330
but it just had like a YAML
file you could give and run

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:01.060
a set of containers
on a single node.

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:05.030
So we moved off of that to a
full Google Kubernetes engine.

00:18:05.030 --> 00:18:08.440
Also, thank you for renaming
it from that to that.

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:10.161
It makes a lot more sense now.

00:18:10.161 --> 00:18:10.660
Yeah.

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:13.350
So GKE is now a big
part of our workflow.

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:16.120
It's enabled us to get
to continuous deployment

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:18.020
really quickly.

00:18:18.020 --> 00:18:21.880
And we are actually doing that
now where as of just a month

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:24.366
or two ago, all of our things
are continuously deployed.

00:18:24.366 --> 00:18:25.990
And we've sort of
moved into that world

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:28.600
very quickly as we've been
able to adopt more Kubernetes

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:29.260
features.

00:18:29.260 --> 00:18:29.660
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:31.493
So I'm just trying to
work through the flow.

00:18:31.493 --> 00:18:33.195
So you said they
have a DOM upload?

00:18:33.195 --> 00:18:34.070
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Yeah.

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:35.460
MARK MANDEL: Where does that go?

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:36.130
MIKE FOTINAKIS: So yeah

00:18:36.130 --> 00:18:36.580
MARK MANDEL: I'm
just trying to--

00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:38.580
MIKE FOTINAKIS: I'll walk
you through the cycle.

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:41.440
So a DOM snapshot gets
uploaded to Percy.

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:43.490
We figure out like,
OK, what browsers

00:18:43.490 --> 00:18:45.130
do you want to render this in?

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:47.637
Because it's a DOM snapshot,
we can also resize the browser

00:18:47.637 --> 00:18:48.220
to different--

00:18:48.220 --> 00:18:49.930
MARK MANDEL: Where does the DOM
snapshot actually go, though?

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:50.830
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Oh,
Google Cloud Storage.

00:18:50.830 --> 00:18:51.190
MARK MANDEL: It goes to storage?

00:18:51.190 --> 00:18:51.350
OK, cool.

00:18:51.350 --> 00:18:51.600
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Sorry.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:52.780
Yeah, it goes to storage.

00:18:52.780 --> 00:18:54.850
So we start in
Google Cloud Storage.

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:59.240
And then, if you think about
it, we actually have to reload.

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:01.180
We have to reconstruct
the state of the page

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:03.370
that you had in your test
environment perfectly

00:19:03.370 --> 00:19:04.850
in our environment.

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:07.270
So what we have to do is
startup Chrome, for example,

00:19:07.270 --> 00:19:10.660
or Firefox, and run a proxy
there to actually say,

00:19:10.660 --> 00:19:14.070
oh, you were loading local
host something index HTML?

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:14.642
Great.

00:19:14.642 --> 00:19:16.600
We have that over here
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:19.630
We proxy all of these
assets using a proxy

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:21.100
that we've written in Go.

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:24.550
And then, we load
up Chrome running

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:27.380
in one of these containers
and we render the page.

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:29.570
We proxy all of your
development assets as well.

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:32.410
So like, we have to do
this because we're not

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:33.790
rendering live sites here.

00:19:33.790 --> 00:19:36.686
We're rendering stuff that's
in CI, in development.

00:19:36.686 --> 00:19:38.060
It's really early
in the process.

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:40.750
So we need to be able
to access and run

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:42.970
things that are your
development assets that

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:44.296
came from your test suite.

00:19:44.296 --> 00:19:45.670
So we can't just
rely on the fact

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:47.950
that your assets might exist
in production somewhere.

00:19:47.950 --> 00:19:51.800
And you might have a CSS
file sitting live accessible.

00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:55.390
So we proxy all of the assets,
including the raw DOM snapshot

00:19:55.390 --> 00:19:56.334
that you gave us.

00:19:56.334 --> 00:19:58.000
And then, potentially
resize the browser

00:19:58.000 --> 00:19:59.380
and do a bunch of
different sizes

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:00.921
that you might have
specified to say,

00:20:00.921 --> 00:20:03.070
we care about this page
on mobile, on desktop,

00:20:03.070 --> 00:20:05.340
on tablet sizes.

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:06.630
Render those screenshots.

00:20:06.630 --> 00:20:09.157
Do the ImageMagick comparison.

00:20:09.157 --> 00:20:09.990
Do the actual diffs.

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:12.180
Upload those, Google
Cloud Storage.

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:14.730
And then, yeah, this
is all sort of--

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:16.460
the back end is all
like a Rails API

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:17.835
too, that's sort
of orchestrating

00:20:17.835 --> 00:20:20.080
some of this process.

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:23.790
And then we also
have a Redis queue

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:25.874
that's helping facilitate
some of the pieces, too.

00:20:25.874 --> 00:20:28.123
MARK MANDEL: Are you running
Kubernetes jobs for this,

00:20:28.123 --> 00:20:29.850
or your own sort of
batch queue process?

00:20:29.850 --> 00:20:31.900
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Our own
batch queue process, yep.

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:32.629
Yeah.

00:20:32.629 --> 00:20:34.920
It's actually great to see
the sort of platform effects

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:36.086
of different providers, too.

00:20:36.086 --> 00:20:39.317
So we like use Redis Labs
for our Redis provider.

00:20:39.317 --> 00:20:40.650
And they just have a GCP option.

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:43.050
So sort of say, GCP, put it into
the same zone we're close to.

00:20:43.050 --> 00:20:43.370
Great.

00:20:43.370 --> 00:20:45.270
We have a Redis host that we
don't have to manage Redis for.

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:45.750
So

00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:47.458
We do that with quite
a bit of our stuff.

00:20:47.458 --> 00:20:49.960
And we use Google Cloud SQL,
which has been really great.

00:20:49.960 --> 00:20:50.760
Yeah.

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:53.190
It's all like MySQL
5.7, or whatever

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:54.860
the latest one is there.

00:20:54.860 --> 00:20:56.250
And yeah, it's
been really great.

00:20:56.250 --> 00:20:58.410
MELANIE WARRICK: And you said
you're also using [INAUDIBLE]??

00:20:58.410 --> 00:20:59.243
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Yes.

00:20:59.243 --> 00:21:02.460
So we recently--
over the last year,

00:21:02.460 --> 00:21:06.910
we've sort of migrated from
heavily using the Google Cloud

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:11.790
HTTPS load balancer to running
our own layer 7 load balancing

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:12.520
stack.

00:21:12.520 --> 00:21:14.060
MARK MANDEL: So why?

00:21:14.060 --> 00:21:16.500
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Good question.

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:18.330
It was sort of driven
from a lot of things.

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:22.069
We wanted to do more, like
traffic splitting experiments.

00:21:22.069 --> 00:21:24.360
We want to be able to like
canary releases a little bit

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:25.420
easier.

00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:28.110
The Google Cloud load balancer
is really great and very

00:21:28.110 --> 00:21:30.930
performant overall, but there
were some features in there

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:33.120
that we started
having some issues

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:35.970
with that we wanted to be able
to have full control over.

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:40.140
So for example, like we want
to be able to internally retry

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:44.040
post requests if they fail,
which the Google Cloud load

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:47.232
balancer only
retries get requests.

00:21:47.232 --> 00:21:49.440
And actually, that was
undocumented at the time, too.

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:51.450
I sort of just only knew that
because I was an ex-Googler

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:52.740
and I was like, oh,
I know that this load

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:54.310
balancer, like
retries get requests,

00:21:54.310 --> 00:21:56.560
but will never retry post
requests because they're not

00:21:56.560 --> 00:21:57.834
[INAUDIBLE].

00:21:57.834 --> 00:22:00.250
So yeah, we wanted to sort of
have more control over that.

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:01.859
And then, as Percy's
infrastructure

00:22:01.859 --> 00:22:03.900
gets more and more complex,
we have more and more

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:05.460
microservices that
sort of fall out.

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:07.440
And like, draw a boundary
around that service

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:09.439
and say, ah, that makes
sense as a microservice.

00:22:09.439 --> 00:22:11.340
So we wanted to be
able to sort of control

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:13.687
the internal RPCs
and how things are

00:22:13.687 --> 00:22:15.270
flowing throughout
the system, and how

00:22:15.270 --> 00:22:16.650
we can orchestrate that stuff.

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:18.270
And as our development
team grows,

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:21.090
give them more ability to just
change one part of the service

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:23.187
rather than changing
a huge monolith.

00:22:23.187 --> 00:22:25.770
MELANIE WARRICK: So since you're
from Google or an ex-Googler,

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:27.750
is that why you went
with GCP to begin with?

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:29.440
MIKE FOTINAKIS:
That was part of it.

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:32.610
I think a lot of it
is because it started

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:33.960
as a side project of mine, too.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:37.170
So I was heavily optimizing
for costs in some ways.

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:40.350
And so my favorite
Google Cloud product

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:42.600
is actually the Google
Cloud Pricing Calculator.

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:46.200
I know that sounds
crazy, but it just

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:47.940
gives me so much
visibility as somebody

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:49.770
who is like paying for it
out of my own bank account

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:51.978
at the time to say, like,
oh, I will know exactly I'm

00:22:51.978 --> 00:22:54.320
going to spend that this
month on this thing.

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:56.160
And so as we were
bootstrapping it early on,

00:22:56.160 --> 00:22:57.868
that gave me a lot of
confidence to know.

00:22:57.868 --> 00:23:01.860
And also, because I knew
that this needed to scale.

00:23:01.860 --> 00:23:03.816
I knew that it just
needed to be able to--

00:23:03.816 --> 00:23:05.190
if you're going
to have something

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:07.890
that can be truly continuous
for everyone's workflow,

00:23:07.890 --> 00:23:09.780
it has to be
horizontally scalable.

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:11.760
You have to just be able
to say, oh, you need

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:13.031
2 million snapshots a month?

00:23:13.031 --> 00:23:13.530
Great.

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:16.230
Here's a bunch of workers
and renderers for you.

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:19.080
You just need that ability based
on the DOM snapshotting model

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:20.730
that we have.

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:22.890
So I sort of bet on
Google Cloud platform

00:23:22.890 --> 00:23:24.890
there because, yeah, it
just made a lot of sense

00:23:24.890 --> 00:23:26.431
for the infrastructure
that we needed

00:23:26.431 --> 00:23:27.900
and the scalability
that we needed.

00:23:27.900 --> 00:23:30.030
And also, as an
ex-Googler, I knew

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:32.790
how good Google's networking
technologies are, for example.

00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:35.600
And just how
world-class that is,

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:37.670
like more than most people know.

00:23:37.670 --> 00:23:39.450
It's just really good.

00:23:39.450 --> 00:23:41.704
MELANIE WARRICK: We did not
feed that to you before.

00:23:41.704 --> 00:23:42.870
MIKE FOTINAKIS: They didn't.

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:45.220
I'm not a Google Cloud
rep at this point.

00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:47.011
MARK MANDEL: Your ice
cream's coming later.

00:23:47.011 --> 00:23:48.340
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Thank you.

00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:49.450
My affiliate fees.

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:51.140
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:23:51.140 --> 00:23:52.650
Well, actually let
me ask then, what

00:23:52.650 --> 00:23:54.540
have been some of
the challenges you've

00:23:54.540 --> 00:23:56.550
hit up against by using GCP?

00:23:56.550 --> 00:23:58.170
MIKE FOTINAKIS:
Well, let me flip

00:23:58.170 --> 00:23:59.295
that question a little bit.

00:23:59.295 --> 00:24:02.190
So like, Kubernetes fills
in a lot of the gaps, right?

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:04.800
If you want to run
your own infrastructure

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:07.169
and orchestrate it--
if you can sort of

00:24:07.169 --> 00:24:08.460
get through the learning curve.

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:09.700
If you can understand the YAML.

00:24:09.700 --> 00:24:11.200
Understand all the
pieces, like what

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:13.860
you're pointing at what
things, then all of a sudden

00:24:13.860 --> 00:24:15.990
you can run your own
infrastructure as a service

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:18.900
and not have a full-time DevOps
person doing it and managing it

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:20.490
every day, or a team.

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:23.070
So it's given us a lot of
flexibility in that way

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:25.295
because we actually
could-- if we needed to,

00:24:25.295 --> 00:24:26.670
we could move off
of Google Cloud

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:28.800
quickly, because
all of these pieces

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:30.000
are sort of cloud-agnostic.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.280
And I know that plays into
sort of Google's like--

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:34.380
we want to really make
the stuff high-quality

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:36.713
and make it so you don't feel
like Cloud lock-in and all

00:24:36.713 --> 00:24:37.500
of this stuff.

00:24:37.500 --> 00:24:39.750
So yeah, some of the challenges.

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:42.150
I guess-- yeah,
getting visibility

00:24:42.150 --> 00:24:44.760
into some of the deeper
inner features of what's

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:47.760
happening inside of the load
balancer and that kind of stuff

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:49.980
can be kind of
challenging sometimes.

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:52.320
That's the biggest
one I can think of.

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:54.809
MELANIE WARRICK: So in terms
of where the product's going,

00:24:54.809 --> 00:24:56.850
what can you share with
us in terms of your plans

00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:58.237
for future outside of--

00:24:58.237 --> 00:24:59.820
you had mentioned
before about wanting

00:24:59.820 --> 00:25:02.450
to be able to directly edit
inside of the visual diffs.

00:25:02.450 --> 00:25:03.800
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Yeah.

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:08.130
So if you think about it,
Percy is all about workflow.

00:25:08.130 --> 00:25:11.140
It's nice to be able
to say like, oh, we

00:25:11.140 --> 00:25:12.140
caught a visual bug.

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:13.640
Oh, we had this
visual bug that kept

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:14.764
happening on our Home page.

00:25:14.764 --> 00:25:16.139
And we got Percy
and we fixed it.

00:25:16.139 --> 00:25:18.222
We're never going to launch
that visual bug again.

00:25:18.222 --> 00:25:18.926
Great.

00:25:18.926 --> 00:25:20.300
Visual regressions
are a big part

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:23.810
of what drives people
to adopt visual testing.

00:25:23.810 --> 00:25:25.970
But the long-term
goal is all about

00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:28.970
facilitating more and
more of those workflows.

00:25:28.970 --> 00:25:31.430
What other pieces can
we pull out and sort of

00:25:31.430 --> 00:25:34.460
automate away from the things
that the software delivery

00:25:34.460 --> 00:25:36.360
lifecycle is doing right now?

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:41.180
So if you think about it,
there's a big gap between,

00:25:41.180 --> 00:25:42.830
I've created a pull request.

00:25:42.830 --> 00:25:44.270
Somebody is reviewing it.

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:47.480
And I'm merging
it to production.

00:25:47.480 --> 00:25:49.980
And that gap can be
a bunch of things.

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:51.499
It can be your
deployment processes,

00:25:51.499 --> 00:25:53.540
like continuous deployment,
if you can get there,

00:25:53.540 --> 00:25:56.430
really helps close
that gap quite a bit.

00:25:56.430 --> 00:25:59.240
But one of the big last pieces
that we just haven't automated

00:25:59.240 --> 00:25:59.870
at all--

00:25:59.870 --> 00:26:02.510
for some reason, like this
it just didn't exist--

00:26:02.510 --> 00:26:04.790
was the ability
to say, like, yes,

00:26:04.790 --> 00:26:07.904
I have full confidence to
know what visual changes are

00:26:07.904 --> 00:26:10.070
going out as part of this
release or as part of this

00:26:10.070 --> 00:26:11.130
commit.

00:26:11.130 --> 00:26:12.930
And so that's really
what we want to do is,

00:26:12.930 --> 00:26:14.690
every piece around
like confidence

00:26:14.690 --> 00:26:16.580
around the deployments
and around those steps

00:26:16.580 --> 00:26:18.050
is what we're aiming to do.

00:26:18.050 --> 00:26:19.730
So you can imagine
you could have

00:26:19.730 --> 00:26:21.320
comments on the visual diffs.

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:24.150
You could say, oh, this
particular part is broken.

00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:25.100
You could collaborate.

00:26:25.100 --> 00:26:27.740
You could say, hey, the
designer on our team,

00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:31.040
she needs to go check this
out and see why that's broken.

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:33.424
Or like, yeah, we have a
bunch of stuff on the roadmap.

00:26:33.424 --> 00:26:35.090
MELANIE WARRICK: And
I can imagine, too.

00:26:35.090 --> 00:26:37.673
I know with machine learning and
the rise of machine learning,

00:26:37.673 --> 00:26:41.720
that the actual UI is constantly
being built to be flexible,

00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:45.915
to change, to be very just
not too hard-coded with.

00:26:45.915 --> 00:26:46.790
MIKE FOTINAKIS: Sure.

00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:48.290
MELANIE WARRICK: So I'd
assume that you're also

00:26:48.290 --> 00:26:50.373
constantly thinking about,
if you're going to do--

00:26:50.373 --> 00:26:52.295
continue to grow
that visual diffs,

00:26:52.295 --> 00:26:55.045
like how that will
adapt as the UI changes.

00:26:55.045 --> 00:26:56.420
MIKE FOTINAKIS:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:26:56.420 --> 00:26:58.460
So machine learning
and computer vision

00:26:58.460 --> 00:27:01.970
can be a big part of what
we can do to automate

00:27:01.970 --> 00:27:03.530
the stuff in the future.

00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:05.210
There's also a big,
hard problem there,

00:27:05.210 --> 00:27:08.030
which is, how do you know if
a visual change is correct

00:27:08.030 --> 00:27:09.229
or not?

00:27:09.229 --> 00:27:10.520
And that's a human part, right?

00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:12.184
Because you can say
as a human, yeah, I

00:27:12.184 --> 00:27:14.600
meant to change that from this
to this or no I didn't, but

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:16.830
getting computers to
understand intent like that

00:27:16.830 --> 00:27:19.299
is a pretty difficult problem.

00:27:19.299 --> 00:27:20.840
So I can see us
going there over time

00:27:20.840 --> 00:27:23.340
as we gather more data around
like, oh, people are approving

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:24.464
things that look like this.

00:27:24.464 --> 00:27:25.899
Or they've hinted
to us that like,

00:27:25.899 --> 00:27:27.440
this is an appropriate
visual change.

00:27:27.440 --> 00:27:28.910
Or we know that
this change looks

00:27:28.910 --> 00:27:32.220
like all of these other changes
and therefore it's also OK.

00:27:32.220 --> 00:27:35.180
And Google Cloud has a bunch of
computer vision APIs and things

00:27:35.180 --> 00:27:37.013
like that, that we might
be able to leverage

00:27:37.013 --> 00:27:38.450
to get closer to
that kind of goal

00:27:38.450 --> 00:27:41.420
where we're presenting to you
the most relevant changes.

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:44.300
Here are the most relevant
visual changes for you.

00:27:44.300 --> 00:27:48.110
And try to knock down the
amount of potential noise

00:27:48.110 --> 00:27:51.470
or the potential for
just an overload of data,

00:27:51.470 --> 00:27:53.300
because you want
these visual reviews

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:56.960
to be as small as possible
and as targeted as possible

00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:58.017
in a lot of ways.

00:27:58.017 --> 00:27:59.600
MELANIE WARRICK: On
a different note--

00:27:59.600 --> 00:28:01.910
I know when we were talking
before we started recording,

00:28:01.910 --> 00:28:03.720
I was saying how I'd be
curious to hear, just

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:05.450
on a little bit of a side
note, what it was like.

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:06.950
I mean, you said
you woke up one day

00:28:06.950 --> 00:28:08.530
and you had the idea for Percy.

00:28:08.530 --> 00:28:11.090
But you didn't just snap your
fingers and you had a company,

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:11.870
I'd assume.

00:28:11.870 --> 00:28:13.760
Unless you did, and
that's pretty impressive.

00:28:13.760 --> 00:28:14.745
What was it like?

00:28:14.745 --> 00:28:16.370
Can you tell us a
little bit about what

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:18.370
it was like going from,
I've got the idea,

00:28:18.370 --> 00:28:20.600
to now I'm actually
running a company.

00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:21.975
MIKE FOTINAKIS:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:28:21.975 --> 00:28:24.219
It sort of started as
like, is this a project?

00:28:24.219 --> 00:28:25.010
Is this a business?

00:28:25.010 --> 00:28:26.720
How big is the opportunity here?

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:28.210
Those kinds of questions.

00:28:28.210 --> 00:28:30.530
I had decided early on to
sort of bootstrap the company

00:28:30.530 --> 00:28:32.720
and see how far I could take it.

00:28:32.720 --> 00:28:35.900
And get some, just
validation around like,

00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:37.304
OK, do people feel this pain?

00:28:37.304 --> 00:28:38.720
Is this just a
pain I was feeling?

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:40.490
Is this a pain everyone feels?

00:28:40.490 --> 00:28:42.890
And so when I started
realizing that like,

00:28:42.890 --> 00:28:46.510
there's really no one that
doesn't have this problem.

00:28:46.510 --> 00:28:48.260
It doesn't matter what
your tech stack is.

00:28:48.260 --> 00:28:51.034
It doesn't matter, really,
what product you're building.

00:28:51.034 --> 00:28:53.450
If you're on the web, or if
you're on mobile, or if you're

00:28:53.450 --> 00:28:56.270
building something that users
are touching or looking at,

00:28:56.270 --> 00:28:58.040
you have visual needs.

00:28:58.040 --> 00:29:00.532
And so then, my co-founder,
Tim, and I joined forces.

00:29:00.532 --> 00:29:02.990
And we were like, OK, let's
take this as a real opportunity

00:29:02.990 --> 00:29:04.892
to build a company around this.

00:29:04.892 --> 00:29:07.100
And that's just been such
a thrilling process for me,

00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:10.550
because this is my first
company and thankfully Tim

00:29:10.550 --> 00:29:12.950
has a lot more
experience in that way.

00:29:12.950 --> 00:29:15.900
We're both engineers by trade,
but he is on multiple companies

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:16.400
now.

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:19.940
So it's good to have him as
sort of my rock in that way

00:29:19.940 --> 00:29:22.910
to help me sort of
realize what's important

00:29:22.910 --> 00:29:25.260
and what's not as we go
through our day to day.

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:26.927
And yeah, it's been
a great experience

00:29:26.927 --> 00:29:28.760
to sort of see like,
even though we're still

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:31.980
a small team, that we can
really execute really quickly.

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:36.080
And that that actually matters
to our customers quite a bit.

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:39.650
So if you're a solo
person working on it,

00:29:39.650 --> 00:29:41.180
you just only have
so much bandwidth

00:29:41.180 --> 00:29:45.020
to address customer needs,
to do sales, to do marketing,

00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:48.770
to do conference talks, to
do all the pieces of it.

00:29:48.770 --> 00:29:50.750
And actually, really
build the machine

00:29:50.750 --> 00:29:54.620
around getting the
info out there.

00:29:54.620 --> 00:29:57.110
So yeah, getting more help and
building the team around it,

00:29:57.110 --> 00:29:59.980
and really realizing that people
are inspired by this problem.

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:01.880
It's a hard thing to solve.

00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:05.704
But when you realize that you
just saved an hour, and then

00:30:05.704 --> 00:30:06.870
you just saved another hour.

00:30:06.870 --> 00:30:08.415
And then you just
saved another hour.

00:30:08.415 --> 00:30:10.440
Every time you're
making these changes,

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:12.030
it's such a drastic
shift in the way

00:30:12.030 --> 00:30:14.992
that even our own team
develops our own software.

00:30:14.992 --> 00:30:16.950
We have a designer who
just joined us recently.

00:30:16.950 --> 00:30:20.040
And you know, he's using-- if
he does a redesign of a certain

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:23.100
part of the app or a
certain way that we're--

00:30:23.100 --> 00:30:24.660
the header or
something on the app.

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:26.160
We have full
confidence that we know

00:30:26.160 --> 00:30:28.560
exactly what is changing
in Percy every time

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:29.830
we change the app.

00:30:29.830 --> 00:30:32.880
So he was making this really
big CSS refactor the other day.

00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:35.160
He was adding all this really
modern CSS stuff, things

00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:36.610
that I didn't even know existed.

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:38.910
And I was like, this is great.

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:42.300
And also, I don't have to even
look at this too carefully

00:30:42.300 --> 00:30:44.740
in some ways because
we know with confidence

00:30:44.740 --> 00:30:47.250
exactly what pixels are going
to change at the end of this.

00:30:47.250 --> 00:30:49.480
So we use Percy as our own
sort of like dogfooding

00:30:49.480 --> 00:30:52.200
to know exactly
what we're changing

00:30:52.200 --> 00:30:53.460
in our own application.

00:30:53.460 --> 00:30:56.460
And it's saved us a bunch of
times where on the billing

00:30:56.460 --> 00:30:58.200
page, we were going
to lose a button that

00:30:58.200 --> 00:31:00.616
was really important that
needed to be there all the time.

00:31:00.616 --> 00:31:02.910
And it was just gone once
because of some change.

00:31:02.910 --> 00:31:04.380
And you know, you catch
that before it even

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:05.150
reaches your customers.

00:31:05.150 --> 00:31:06.810
So that kind of
stuff has been really

00:31:06.810 --> 00:31:08.910
fun to see as we built
the company side of it,

00:31:08.910 --> 00:31:11.310
like our customers have
those same problems,

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:14.370
have those same issues, no
matter what their tech stack,

00:31:14.370 --> 00:31:15.990
what their sort of environment.

00:31:15.990 --> 00:31:18.817
If they care about the
design or the stability

00:31:18.817 --> 00:31:20.400
of their application
at all, then they

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:23.160
care about the visual
side of their sites.

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:25.590
So yeah, that's been a
really thrilling experience.

00:31:25.590 --> 00:31:27.720
And I also have
learned that I love

00:31:27.720 --> 00:31:29.562
wearing multiple
hats all the time

00:31:29.562 --> 00:31:31.020
and sort of switching
between them.

00:31:31.020 --> 00:31:32.194
It can be very--

00:31:32.194 --> 00:31:34.110
I don't know, like a lot
of emotional overhead

00:31:34.110 --> 00:31:35.760
can go into being the
founder, which is something

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:37.930
that I'm learning and
sort of anticipating.

00:31:37.930 --> 00:31:40.080
So it's a lot of emotional
context switching.

00:31:40.080 --> 00:31:42.840
You need to be able to be
in a meeting at once that's

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:45.150
sort of like a drag or
is like really hard,

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:46.500
and then you need to
move to the next meeting

00:31:46.500 --> 00:31:47.280
and be really happy.

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:48.946
And you need to move
to the next meeting

00:31:48.946 --> 00:31:50.421
and not say that one thing.

00:31:50.421 --> 00:31:52.170
It's just emotional
context switching over

00:31:52.170 --> 00:31:52.920
and over and over.

00:31:52.920 --> 00:31:55.860
So it can be thrilling if you
have the right team behind you

00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:57.885
and really inspired to
work on the product.

00:31:57.885 --> 00:31:59.010
And really, make it better.

